    {
      "paperId": "SCOPUS-PUBLICATION:105013740372",
      "doi": "",
      "title": "Towards a robust calibration and model discrepancy reduction for digital twin spacecraft: application to European Space Agency (ESA) Cluster mission",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Sgorlon Gaiatto C."
        },
        {
          "name": "Antonello F."
        },
        {
          "name": "Segneri D."
        },
        {
          "name": "Maass J."
        },
        {
          "name": "Inojosa A."
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The development of a Digital Twin (DT) for spacecraft represents a key step in the digital transformation of space systems, which has the potential to enable high fidelity simulation capabilities, enhanced mission situational awareness, procedure validation, autonomous operations and predictive maintenance, to mention a few. A DT relies on simulation models with configurable parameters that must be calibrated to accurately represent the behaviour of the real system. Commonly used approaches for the calibration of spacecraft simulation models are optimization-based methods, such as numerical techniques and evolutionary algorithms. However, they do not incorporate uncertainty quantification (UQ), which is a fundamental requirement for the development of accurate and reliable twinning technologies. In this light, we propose a Bayesian calibration framework that accounts for various sources of uncertainty, including model parameter uncertainty, model form uncertainty, and observation uncertainty. It provides posterior estimates for model parameters and credible intervals for model predictions. Also, the framework is applied to the real data of European Space Agency (ESA)’s Cluster mission to calibrate a predictive model for solar array degradation and power generation. The results indicate that the proposed approach provides robust model calibration under uncertainty and effectively reduces model discrepancy, showing significant improvements over previous approaches.",
